2. Application Optimization Characteristics
Jacobi-2D repeatedly updates a 2D grid over multiple time steps using neighboring points and alternates between two arrays. Spatial points inside a single update phase are parallel, but time steps and the A↔B phases carry dependencies. Performance is typically memory-bandwidth and locality sensitive, so spatial tiling, temporal/wavefront tiling, thread reuse, and avoiding excessive synchronization are central.
3. Approach-by-Approach Knowledge
Approach	Observed optimization	Speedup advantages	Speedup disadvantages
PPCG – OpenMP	Transforms the stencil into a dependence-aware skewed iteration space, but the generated untiled form contains no visible OpenMP parallel directive in the inspected kernel.	Legality-aware affine transformation preserves time/space dependences and creates a basis for more advanced wavefront execution.	Untiled transformed code is complex while exposing little direct thread parallelism; no explicit SIMD hints; may add index arithmetic without sufficient locality benefit.
PPCG – Tiled	Applies 32-sized tiling over the skewed time/space iteration space.	Temporal/spatial blocking can improve cache reuse across neighboring stencil updates and reduce memory traffic; dependence-aware tiles preserve legal execution order.	Generated bounds are highly complex; no explicit OpenMP directive is visible in this tiled PPCG kernel; fixed tile sizes may not match cache capacity; control overhead can offset gains for small grids/time steps.
PLUTO – Original	Uses time skewing/wavefront-style affine scheduling and OpenMP parallel loops over legal transformed dimensions.	Exposes legal parallel wavefronts across time/space; can increase parallelism while respecting dependencies; better opportunity for reuse than naive per-time-step execution.	Complex schedule/bounds; frequent synchronization between wavefronts; no simple source-level locality model; performance can be sensitive to problem dimensions and OpenMP overhead.
PLUTO – Tiled	Adds multidimensional tiling to the skewed schedule and retains OpenMP wavefront parallelism.	Potentially strong temporal locality by reusing grid values within tiles; combines cache blocking and legal cross-time parallelism; can reduce main-memory traffic.	Very large and complex control structure; boundary handling is expensive; fixed tiles can create load imbalance; more difficult for downstream compiler analysis.
Gemini	For each time step, parallelizes the outer i loop separately for the A→B and B→A sweeps.	Simple and safe spatial parallelism; contiguous inner-j traversal is cache-friendly; low code complexity.	Creates two parallel regions per time step, causing repeated fork/join and barriers; no collapse, temporal tiling, or SIMD; does not reuse a persistent thread team.
GPT-5.2	Adds collapse(2) and schedule(static) to both spatial sweeps at every time step.	More parallel iterations are exposed; static scheduling is predictable and low overhead for uniform stencil work; useful if N is too small to saturate threads with the outer loop alone.	Still creates two parallel regions per time step; collapse(2) may reduce row locality and increase scheduling granularity; no temporal tiling; synchronization remains on every sweep.
4. Cross-Approach Synthesis
The compiler-generated variants emphasize dependence-aware affine transformations and, in their tiled versions, explicit locality optimization. The LLM-generated variants generally favor simpler OpenMP edits that preserve the original program structure. This simplicity can reduce transformation overhead and improve maintainability, while PPCG/PLUTO can expose locality and legal parallelism that are difficult to derive from a local pragma insertion alone.
A strong SmartCompiler-style strategy should therefore combine the two: retain compiler legality/dependence knowledge for tiling and scheduling, but prefer the simplest parallel structure that achieves the required locality and thread utilization on the target machine.
5. Reusable Expert Knowledge for Speedup
Keep time-step ordering and the A→B/B→A phase barrier intact; parallelize only dependence-free spatial work unless using a proven wavefront transformation.
Use a persistent OpenMP parallel region around the time loop and omp for inside each phase to reduce repeated fork/join overhead.
Prefer static scheduling for the regular stencil workload.
Use spatial tiling first for cache locality; use temporal/wavefront tiling only when the implementation can preserve dependencies and amortize its control overhead.
Avoid collapse(2) by default when row-wise locality already gives sufficient parallelism; enable it when outer rows alone underutilize available threads.
Vectorize the contiguous inner dimension and choose tile dimensions that preserve contiguous access.
6. Knowledge Guidance for SmartCompiler
When optimizing this application, use the rules above as constraints and preferences rather than blindly copying any one generated version. Select transformations according to matrix/grid size, thread count, cache hierarchy, vector width, and measured overhead. Favor a hybrid candidate that preserves compiler-proven legality while reducing unnecessary parallel-region creation, complex cleanup logic, and fine-grained scheduling.

